@ 36.3.1.2 - Proto-Naish to Mosuo (Na)
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Jacques, Guillaume, and Alexis Michaud (2011), “Approaching the historical phonology of three highly eroded Sino-Tibetan languages: Naxi, Na and Laze”. Diachronica 28:4 (2011), 468 – 498
	a > e / :{ S_%, P:[-voice]_% }:
	a > wɤ / {[+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo],ŋ}w_%
	a > i // [+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo]_%
	iN > æ / {P,C}r_
	{ {iN,u}, i } > { i, ɯ } / P:[-voice]_
	i > ɯ / :{ S_, [+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo]r_ }:
	i > v̩ / m_
	{ u, o } > { v̩, u }
	[+back] > ɔ / :{ qʰ_, (N)q_ }:
	V% > high rising
	{Np,mb} > b / _V
	{r,s}pʰ {r,s}{(N)p,(m)b} > pʰ p b / _V (the paper implies similar development͡s occurred with stops at other PO:[+affricate]s)
	{ {r,s}k, {r,s}Nk } > { k, ʁ } / _V
	S{b,ɡ} > ∅
	Sk > h
	n̥ > l̥ / %_V
	N̥V > hṼ
	l̥ > ɬ / %_V